h3 2.3.5.6 Time zones
text A  consists of a signed number of hours and minutes.
text time-zone offset
text A string is a  representing a time-zone offset if it consists of either:
text valid time-zone offset string
text A U+005A LATIN CAPITAL LETTER Z character (Z), allowed only if the time zone is UTC
text Or, the following components, in the given order:
text Either a U+002B PLUS SIGN character (+) or, if the time-zone offset is not zero, a U+002D HYPHEN-MINUS character (-), representing the sign of the time-zone offset
text Two , representing the hours component  of the time-zone offset, in the range 0 ≤  ≤ 23
row
  link "ASCII digits" https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-digit
  text hour
  text hour
text Optionally, a U+003A COLON character (:)
text Two , representing the minutes component  of the time-zone offset, in the range 0 ≤  ≤ 59
row
  link "ASCII digits" https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-digit
  text minute
  text minute
text This format allows for time-zone offsets from -23:59 to +23:59. Right now, in practice, the range of offsets of actual time zones is -12:00 to +14:00, and the minutes component of offsets of actual time zones is always either 00, 30, or 45. There is no guarantee that this will remain so forever, however, since time zones are used as political footballs and are thus subject to very whimsical policy decisions. success
text See also the usage notes and examples in the  section below for details on using time-zone offsets with historical times that predate the formation of formal time zones. success
