h3 2.3.5.7 Global dates and times
text A  consists of a specific , consisting of a year, a month, and a day, and a time, consisting of an hour, a minute, a second, and a fraction of a second, expressed with a time-zone offset, consisting of a signed number of hours and minutes.
text global date and time
link "proleptic-Gregorian date" #proleptic-gregorian-date
link [GREGORIAN] #refsGREGORIAN
text A string is a  representing a date, time, and a time-zone offset if it consists of the following components in the given order:
text valid global date and time string
text A  representing the date
link "valid date string" #valid-date-string
text A U+0054 LATIN CAPITAL LETTER T character (T) or a U+0020 SPACE character
text A  representing the time
link "valid time string" #valid-time-string
text A  representing the time-zone offset
link "valid time-zone offset string" #valid-time-zone-offset-string
text Times in dates before the formation of UTC in the mid-twentieth century must be expressed and interpreted in terms of UT1 (contemporary Earth solar time at the 0° longitude), not UTC (the approximation of UT1 that ticks in SI seconds). Time before the formation of time zones must be expressed and interpreted as UT1 times with explicit time zones that approximate the contemporary difference between the appropriate local time and the time observed at the location of Greenwich, London.
text The following are some examples of dates written as .
link "valid global date and time strings" #valid-global-date-and-time-string
text "\"0037-12-13 00:00Z\""
text Midnight in areas using London time on the birthday of Nero (the Roman Emperor). See below for further discussion on which date this actually corresponds to.
text "\"1979-10-14T12:00:00.001-04:00\""
text One millisecond after noon on October 14th 1979, in the time zone in use on the east coast of the USA during daylight saving time.
text "\"8592-01-01T02:09+02:09\""
text Midnight UTC on the 1st of January, 8592. The time zone associated with that time is two hours and nine minutes ahead of UTC, which is not currently a real time zone, but is nonetheless allowed.
text Several things are notable about these dates:
text "Years with fewer than four digits have to be zero-padded. The date \"37-12-13\" would not be a valid date."
text "If the \"T\" is replaced by a space, it must be a single space character. The string \"2001-12-21 12:00Z\" (with two spaces between the components) would not be parsed successfully."
text To unambiguously identify a moment in time prior to the introduction of the Gregorian calendar (insofar as moments in time before the formation of UTC can be unambiguously identified), the date has to be first converted to the Gregorian calendar from the calendar in use at the time (e.g. from the Julian calendar). The date of Nero's birth is the 15th of December 37, in the Julian Calendar, which is the 13th of December 37 in the .
link "proleptic Gregorian calendar" #proleptic-gregorian-calendar
text The time and time-zone offset components are not optional.
text Dates before the year one can't be represented as a datetime in this version of HTML.
text Times of specific events in ancient times are, at best, approximations, since time was not well coordinated or measured until relatively recent decades.
text Time-zone offsets differ based on daylight saving time.
text "The rules to  are as follows. This will return either a time in UTC, with associated time-zone offset information for roundtripping or display purposes, or nothing. If at any point the algorithm says that it \"fails\", this means that it is aborted at that point and returns nothing."
