text The  element  some , optionally with a caption, that is self-contained (like a complete sentence) and is typically  as a single unit from the main flow of the document.
link figure #the-figure-element
link represents #represents
link "flow content" #flow-content-2
link referenced #referenced
text "\"Self-contained\" in this context does not necessarily mean independent. For example, each sentence in a paragraph is self-contained; an image that is part of a sentence would be inappropriate for , but an entire sentence made of images would be fitting." success
link figure #the-figure-element
text The element can thus be used to annotate illustrations, diagrams, photos, code listings, etc.
text When a  is referred to from the main content of the document by identifying it by its caption (e.g., by figure number), it enables such content to be easily moved away from that primary content, e.g., to the side of the page, to dedicated pages, or to an appendix, without affecting the flow of the document. success
link figure #the-figure-element
text "If a  element is  by its relative position, e.g., \"in the photograph above\" or \"as the next figure shows\", then moving the figure would disrupt the page's meaning. Authors are encouraged to consider using labels to refer to figures, rather than using such relative references, so that the page can easily be restyled without affecting the page's meaning." success
row
  link figure #the-figure-element
  link referenced #referenced
text The first  element child of the element, if any, represents the caption of the  element's contents. If there is no child  element, then there is no caption.
row
  link figcaption #the-figcaption-element
  link figure #the-figure-element
  link figcaption #the-figcaption-element
text A  element's contents are part of the surrounding flow. If the purpose of the page is to display the figure, for example a photograph on an image sharing site, the  and  elements can be used to explicitly provide a caption for that figure. For content that is only tangentially related, or that serves a separate purpose than the surrounding flow, the  element should be used (and can itself wrap a ). For example, a pull quote that repeats content from an  would be more appropriate in an  than in a , because it isn't part of the content, it's a repetition of the content for the purposes of enticing readers or highlighting key topics.
