text The level of stress that a particular piece of content has is given by its number of ancestor  elements.
link em #the-em-element
text The placement of stress emphasis changes the meaning of the sentence. The element thus forms an integral part of the content. The precise way in which stress is used in this way depends on the language.
text These examples show how changing the stress emphasis changes the meaning. First, a general statement of fact, with no stress:
text Cats are cute animals.
card
  text <
  text p success
  text >
  text </
  text p success
  text >
text By emphasizing the first word, the statement implies that the kind of animal under discussion is in question (maybe someone is asserting that dogs are cute):
text Cats are cute animals.
card
  text <
  text p success
  text ><
  text em success
  text >
  text </
  text em success
  text >
  text </
  text p success
  text >
text Moving the stress to the verb, one highlights that the truth of the entire sentence is in question (maybe someone is saying cats are not cute):
text Cats are cute animals.
card
  text <
  text p success
  text >
  text <
  text em success
  text >
  text </
  text em success
  text >
  text </
  text p success
  text >
text By moving it to the adjective, the exact nature of the cats is reasserted (maybe someone suggested cats were mean animals):
text Cats are cute animals.
card
  text <
  text p success
  text >
  text <
  text em success
  text >
  text </
  text em success
  text >
  text </
  text p success
  text >
text Similarly, if someone asserted that cats were vegetables, someone correcting this might emphasize the last word:
text Cats are cute animals.
