text However, there are a number of situations for which the author might wish to use multiple image resources that the user agent can choose from:
text Different users might have different environmental characteristics:
text The users' physical screen size might be different from one another.
text A mobile phone's screen might be 4 inches diagonally, while a laptop's screen might be 14 inches diagonally.
region "The phone's screen is much smaller compared to the laptop's screen." 500x200
  rect @6,18 6x12 fill:#ffffff stroke:#000000 radius:1
  text @9,24 "4″" size:2.5 anchor:middle
  rect @30,2 40x26 fill:#ffffff stroke:#000000 width:2 radius:2
  line @26,30 @74,30 stroke:#000000 width:2
  text @50,15 "14″" size:2.5 anchor:middle
text This is only relevant when an image's rendered size depends on the  size. success
link viewport https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport
text The users' screen pixel density might be different from one another.
text A mobile phone's screen might have three times as many physical pixels per inch compared to another mobile phone's screen, regardless of their physical screen size.
region "One phone has big pixels, the other has small pixels." 352.58x170
  rect @6,8 6x12 fill:#ffffff stroke:#000000 radius:1
  rect @36,8 6x12 fill:#ffffff stroke:#000000 radius:1
  line @15,16 @22,24 stroke:#a52a2a width:3
  circle @10,10 r:8 fill:url(#img-intro-pixel) stroke:#000000
  line @45,16 @52,24 stroke:#a52a2a width:3
  circle @40,10 r:8 fill:url(#img-intro-pixel) stroke:#000000 width:3
  text @20,10 "1x" size:2.5
  text @50,10 "3x" size:2.5
text The users' zoom level might be different from one another, or might change for a single user over time.
text A user might zoom in to a particular image to be able to get a more detailed look.
text The zoom level and the screen pixel density (the previous point) can both affect the number of physical screen pixels per . This ratio is usually referred to as .
link "CSS pixel" https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values/#px
text device-pixel-ratio
text The users' screen orientation might be different from one another, or might change for a single user over time.
text "A tablet can be held upright or rotated 90 degrees, so that the screen is either \"portrait\" or \"landscape\"."
region "The tablet has two orientations." 375x200
  rect @6,5 14x20 fill:#ffffff stroke:#000000 radius:1
  line @6,24 @20,24 stroke:#000000 width:1.1
  text @13,14.5 "Portrait" size:2.5 anchor:middle
  rect @30,11 20x14 fill:#ffffff stroke:#000000 radius:1
  line @31,25 @31,11 stroke:#000000 width:1.1
  text @40.5,18 "Landscape" size:2.5 anchor:middle
text The users' network speed, network latency and bandwidth cost might be different from one another, or might change for a single user over time.
text A user might be on a fast, low-latency and constant-cost connection while at work, on a slow, low-latency and constant-cost connection while at home, and on a variable-speed, high-latency and variable-cost connection anywhere else.
text Authors might want to show the same image content but with different rendered size depending on, usually, the width of the . This is usually referred to as .
link viewport https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport
text viewport-based selection
text A web page might have a banner at the top that always spans the entire  width. In this case, the rendered size of the image depends on the physical size of the screen (assuming a maximised browser window).
