h3 4.8.4.4 Requirements for providing text to act as an alternative for images
h3 4.8.4.4.1 General guidelines
text Except where otherwise specified, the  attribute must be specified and its value must not be empty; the value must be an appropriate replacement for the image. The specific requirements for the  attribute depend on what the image is intended to represent, as described in the following sections.
link alt #attr-img-alt
link alt #attr-img-alt
text The most general rule to consider when writing alternative text is the following: .
text the intent is that replacing every image with the text of its  attribute does not change the meaning of the page
link alt #attr-img-alt
text So, in general, alternative text can be written by considering what one would have written had one not been able to include the image.
text A corollary to this is that the  attribute's value should never contain text that could be considered the image's , , or . It is supposed to contain replacement text that could be used by users  of the image; it is not meant to supplement the image. The  attribute can be used for supplemental information.
link alt #attr-img-alt
text caption
text title
text legend
text instead
link title #attr-title
text Another corollary is that the  attribute's value should not repeat information that is already provided in the prose next to the image.
link alt #attr-img-alt
text One way to think of alternative text is to think about how you would read the page containing the image to someone over the phone, without mentioning that there is an image present. Whatever you say instead of the image is typically a good start for writing the alternative text. success
h3 4.8.4.4.2 A link or button containing nothing but the image
text When an  element that creates a , or a  element, has no textual content but contains one or more images, the  attributes must contain text that together convey the purpose of the link or button.
row
  link a #the-a-element
  link hyperlink #hyperlink
  link button #the-button-element
  link alt #attr-img-alt
