h3 4.8.4.4.3 A phrase or paragraph with an alternative graphical representation: charts, diagrams, graphs, maps, illustrations
text Sometimes something can be more clearly stated in graphical form, for example as a flowchart, a diagram, a graph, or a simple map showing directions. In such cases, an image can be given using the  element, but the lesser textual version must still be given, so that users who are unable to view the image (e.g. because they have a very slow connection, or because they are using a text-only browser, or because they are listening to the page being read out by a hands-free automobile voice web browser, or simply because they are blind) are still able to understand the message being conveyed.
link img #the-img-element
text The text must be given in the  attribute, and must convey the same message as the image specified in the  attribute.
row
  link alt #attr-img-alt
  link src #attr-img-src
text It is important to realize that the alternative text is a replacement for the image, not a description of the image.
text In the following example we have  in image form, with text in the  attribute rephrasing the flowchart in prose form:
row
  link "a flowchart" /images/parsing-model-overview.svg
  link alt #attr-img-alt
text In the common case, the data handled by the tokenization stage comes from the network, but it can also come from script.
card
  text <
  text p success
  text >
  text </
  text p success
  text >
