text Many pages include logos, insignia, flags, or emblems, which stand for a particular entity such as a company, organization, project, band, software package, country, or some such.
text "If the logo is being used to represent the entity, e.g. as a page heading, the  attribute must contain the name of the entity being represented by the logo. The  attribute must  contain text like the word \"logo\", as it is not the fact that it is a logo that is being conveyed, it's the entity itself."
link alt #attr-img-alt
link alt #attr-img-alt
text not
text If the logo is being used next to the name of the entity that it represents, then the logo is supplemental, and its  attribute must instead be empty.
link alt #attr-img-alt
text If the logo is merely used as decorative material (as branding, or, for example, as a side image in an article that mentions the entity to which the logo belongs), then the entry below on purely decorative images applies. If the logo is actually being discussed, then it is being used as a phrase or paragraph (the description of the logo) with an alternative graphical representation (the logo itself), and the first entry above applies.
