text This example shows how the alternative text should be written such that if the image isn't , and the text is used instead, the text flows seamlessly into the surrounding text, as if the image had never been there in the first place.
link available #img-available
h3 4.8.4.4.5 Text that has been rendered to a graphic for typographical effect
text Sometimes, an image just consists of text, and the purpose of the image is not to highlight the actual typographic effects used to render the text, but just to convey the text itself.
text In such cases, the  attribute must be present but must consist of the same text as written in the image itself.
link alt #attr-img-alt
text "Consider a graphic containing the text \"Earth Day\", but with the letters all decorated with flowers and plants. If the text is merely being used as a heading, to spice up the page for graphical users, then the correct alternative text is just the same text \"Earth Day\", and no mention need be made of the decorations:"
card
  text <
  text h1 success
  text >
  row
    text <
    text img success
    text src primary
    text =
    text "\"earthdayheading.png\"" warning
    text alt primary
    text =
    text "\"Earth Day\"" warning
    text >
  text </
  text h1 success
  text >
text An illuminated manuscript might use graphics for some of its images. The alternative text in such a situation is just the character that the image represents.
text nce upon a time and a long long time ago, late at night, when it was dark, over the hills, through the woods, across a great ocean, in a land far away, in a small house, on a hill, under a full moon...
card
