h3 4.8.4.4.7 Ancillary images
text Sometimes, an image is not critical to the content, but is nonetheless neither purely decorative nor entirely redundant with the text. In these cases, the  attribute must be present, and its value should either be the empty string, or a textual representation of the information that the image conveys. If the image has a caption giving the image's title, then the  attribute's value must not be empty (as that would be quite confusing for non-visual readers).
link alt #attr-img-alt
link alt #attr-img-alt
text Consider a news article about a political figure, in which the individual's face was shown in an image. The image is not purely decorative, as it is relevant to the story. The image is not entirely redundant with the story either, as it shows what the politician looks like. Whether any alternative text need be provided is an authoring decision, decided by whether the image influences the interpretation of the prose.
text In this first variant, the image is shown without context, and no alternative text is provided:
text Ahead of today's referendum, the President wrote an open letter to all registered voters. In it, she admitted that the country was divided.
card
  text <
  text p success
  text >
  row
    text <
    text img success
    text src primary
    text =
    text "\"president.jpeg\"" warning
    text alt primary
    text =
    text "\"\"" warning
    text >
  text </
  text p success
  text >
text If the picture is just a face, there might be no value in describing it. It's of no interest to the reader whether the individual has red hair or blond hair, whether the individual has white skin or black skin, whether the individual has one eye or two eyes.
text However, if the picture is more dynamic, for instance showing the politician as angry, or particularly happy, or devastated, some alternative text would be useful in setting the tone of the article, a tone that might otherwise be missed:
text Ahead of today's referendum, the President wrote an open letter to all registered voters. In it, she admitted that the country was divided.
card
  text <
  text p success
  text >
  row
    text <
    text img success
    text src primary
    text =
    text "\"president.jpeg\"" warning
    text alt primary
    text =
    text "\"The President is sad.\"" warning
    text >
  text </
  text p success
  text >
text Ahead of today's referendum, the President wrote an open letter to all registered voters. In it, she admitted that the country was divided.
card
  text <
  text p success
  text >
  row
    text <
    text img success
    text src primary
    text =
    text "\"president.jpeg\"" warning
    text alt primary
    text =
    text "\"The President is happy!\"" warning
    text >
  text </
  text p success
  text >
text "Whether the individual was \"sad\" or \"happy\" makes a difference to how the rest of the paragraph is to be interpreted: is she likely saying that she is unhappy with the country being divided, or is she saying that the prospect of a divided country is good for her political career? The interpretation varies based on the image."
text If the image has a caption, then including alternative text avoids leaving the non-visual user confused as to what the caption refers to.
text Ahead of today's referendum, the President wrote an open letter to all registered voters. In it, she admitted that the country was divided.
card
  text <
  text p success
  text >
  text </
  text p success
  text >
  text The President of Ruritania. Photo © 2014 PolitiPhoto. success
