  text "\"A high forehead, cheerful disposition, and dark hair round out the President's face.\"" warning
  text >
  text <
  text figcaption success
  text >
  text </
  text figcaption success
  text >
  text </
  text figure success
  text >
h3 4.8.4.4.8 A purely decorative image that doesn't add any information
text If an image is decorative but isn't especially page-specific — for example an image that forms part of a site-wide design scheme — the image should be specified in the site's CSS, not in the markup of the document.
text However, a decorative image that isn't discussed by the surrounding text but still has some relevance can be included in a page using the  element. Such images are decorative, but still form part of the content. In these cases, the  attribute must be present but its value must be the empty string.
link img #the-img-element
link alt #attr-img-alt
text Examples where the image is purely decorative despite being relevant would include things like a photo of the Black Rock City landscape in a blog post about an event at Burning Man, or an image of a painting inspired by a poem, on a page reciting that poem. The following snippet shows an example of the latter case (only the first verse is included in this snippet):
text The Lady of Shalott  On either side the river lie Long fields of barley and of rye, That clothe the wold and meet the sky; And through the field the road run by To many-tower'd Camelot; And up and down the people go, Gazing where the lilies blow Round an island there below, The island of Shalott.
