  text "with each year: 624%, 75%, 138%, 40%, 35%, 9%, 21%\"" warning
  text >
text "Note that \"sales graph\" would be inadequate alternative text for a sales graph. Text that would be a good caption is not generally suitable as replacement text."
text Images that defy a complete description
text In certain cases, the nature of the image might be such that providing thorough alternative text is impractical. For example, the image could be indistinct, or could be a complex fractal, or could be a detailed topographical map.
text In these cases, the  attribute must contain some suitable alternative text, but it may be somewhat brief.
link alt #attr-img-alt
text Sometimes there simply is no text that can do justice to an image. For example, there is little that can be said to usefully describe a Rorschach inkblot test. However, a description, even if brief, is still better than nothing:
text A black outline of the first of the ten cards in the Rorschach inkblot test.
card
  text <
  text figure success
  text >
  text <
  text img success
  text src primary
  text =
  text "\"/commons/a/a7/Rorschach1.jpg\"" warning
  text alt primary
  text =
  text "\"A shape with left-right" warning
  text symmetry with indistinct edges, with a small gap in the center, two warning
  text larger gaps offset slightly from the center, with two similar gaps warning
  text under them. The outline is wider in the top half than the bottom warning
  text half, with the sides extending upwards higher than the center, and warning
  text "the center extending below the sides.\"" warning
  text >
  text <
  text figcaption success
  text >
  text </
  text figcaption success
  text >
  text </
  text figure success
  text >
text Note that the following would be a very bad use of alternative text:
text A black outline of the first of the ten cards in the Rorschach inkblot test.
card
  text <!-- This example is wrong. Do not copy it. -->
  text <
  text figure
  text >
  text <
  text img
  text src
  text =
  text "\"/commons/a/a7/Rorschach1.jpg\""
  text alt
  text =
  text "\"A black outline"
  text "of the first of the ten cards in the Rorschach inkblot test.\""
  text >
  text <
  text figcaption
  text >
  text </
  text figcaption
  text >
  text </
  text figure
  text >
text Including the caption in the alternative text like this isn't useful because it effectively duplicates the caption for users who don't have images, taunting them twice yet not helping them any more than if they had only read or heard the caption once.
text Another example of an image that defies full description is a fractal, which, by definition, is infinite in detail.
text The following example shows one possible way of providing alternative text for the full view of an image of the Mandelbrot set.
card
  text <
  text img success
  text src primary
  text =
  text "\"ms1.jpeg\"" warning
  text alt primary
  text =
  text "\"The Mandelbrot set appears as a cardioid with" warning
  text its cusp on the real axis in the positive direction, with a smaller warning
  text bulb aligned along the same center line, touching it in the negative warning
  text direction, and with these two shapes being surrounded by smaller bulbs warning
  text "of various sizes.\"" warning
  text >
text Similarly, a photograph of a person's face, for example in a biography, can be considered quite relevant and key to the content, but it can be hard to fully substitute text for:
text A Biography of Isaac Asimov Born Isaak Yudovich Ozimov in 1920, Isaac was a prolific author.     Asimov was born in Russia, and moved to the US when he was three years old. ...
card
  text <
  text section success
  text class primary
  text =
  text "\"bio\"" warning
  text >
  text <
  text h1 success
  text >
  text </
  text h1 success
  text >
  text <
  text p success
  text >
  text <
  text b success
  text >
  text </
  text b success
  text >
  text </
  text p success
  text >
  text <
  text p success
  text ><
  text img success
  text src primary
  text =
  text "\"headpics/asimov.jpeg\"" warning
  text alt primary
  text =
  text "\"Isaac Asimov had dark hair, a tall forehead, and wore glasses." warning
  text "Later in life, he wore long white sideburns.\"" warning
  text ></
  text p success
  text >
  text <
  text p success
  text >
  text </
  text p success
  text >
  text <
  text p success
  text >
  text </
  text p success
  text >
  text </
  text section success
  text >
text "In such cases it is unnecessary (and indeed discouraged) to include a reference to the presence of the image itself in the alternative text, since such text would be redundant with the browser itself reporting the presence of the image. For example, if the alternative text was \"A photo of Isaac Asimov\", then a conforming user agent might read that out as \"(Image) A photo of Isaac Asimov\" rather than the more useful \"(Image) Isaac Asimov had dark hair, a tall forehead, and wore glasses...\"."
text Images whose contents are not known
text In some unfortunate cases, there might be no alternative text available at all, either because the image is obtained in some automated fashion without any associated alternative text (e.g., a webcam), or because the page is being generated by a script using user-provided images where the user did not provide suitable or usable alternative text (e.g. photograph sharing sites), or because the author does not themself know what the images represent (e.g. a blind photographer sharing an image on their blog).
text In such cases, the  attribute may be omitted, but one of the following conditions must be met as well:
link alt #attr-img-alt
text The  element is in a  element that contains a  element that contains content other than , and, ignoring the  element and its descendants, the  element has no  descendants other than  and the  element.
link img #the-img-element
link figure #the-figure-element
link figcaption #the-figcaption-element
link "inter-element whitespace" #inter-element-whitespace
link figcaption #the-figcaption-element
link figure #the-figure-element
link "flow content" #flow-content-2
link "inter-element whitespace" #inter-element-whitespace
link img #the-img-element
text The  attribute is present and has a non-empty value.
link title #attr-title
text Relying on the  attribute is currently discouraged as many user agents do not expose the attribute in an accessible manner as required by this specification (e.g. requiring a pointing device such as a mouse to cause a tooltip to appear, which excludes keyboard-only users and touch-only users, such as anyone with a modern phone or tablet). success
link title #attr-title
text Such cases are to be kept to an absolute minimum. If there is even the slightest possibility of the author having the ability to provide real alternative text, then it would not be acceptable to omit the  attribute. success
link alt #attr-img-alt
text A photo on a photo-sharing site, if the site received the image with no metadata other than the caption, could be marked up as follows:
text Bubbles traveled everywhere with us.
