h3 4.10.1.2 Implementing the server-side processing for a form
text This section is non-normative.
text The exact details for writing a server-side processor are out of scope for this specification. For the purposes of this introduction, we will assume that the script at  is configured to accept submissions using the  format, expecting the following parameters sent in an HTTP POST body:
row
  text "https://pizza.example.com/order.cgi"
  link application/x-www-form-urlencoded #attr-fs-enctype-urlencoded
text custname
text Customer's name
text custtel
text Customer's telephone number
text custemail
text Customer's email address
text size
text The pizza size, either small, medium, or large
text topping
text A topping, specified once for each selected topping, with the allowed values being bacon, cheese, onion, and mushroom
text delivery
text The requested delivery time
text comments
text The delivery instructions
h3 4.10.1.3 Configuring a form to communicate with a server
text This section is non-normative.
text Form submissions are exposed to servers in a variety of ways, most commonly as HTTP GET or POST requests. To specify the exact method used, the  attribute is specified on the  element. This doesn't specify how the form data is encoded, though; to specify that, you use the  attribute. You also have to specify the  of the service that will handle the submitted data, using the  attribute.
link method #attr-fs-method
link form #the-form-element
link enctype #attr-fs-enctype
link URL https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url
link action #attr-fs-action
text For each form control you want submitted, you then have to give a name that will be used to refer to the data in the submission. We already specified the name for the group of radio buttons; the same attribute () also specifies the submission name. Radio buttons can be distinguished from each other in the submission by giving them different values, using the  attribute.
link name #attr-fe-name
link value #attr-input-value
text Multiple controls can have the same name; for example, here we give all the checkboxes the same name, and the server distinguishes which checkbox was checked by seeing which values are submitted with that name — like the radio buttons, they are also given unique values with the  attribute.
link value #attr-input-value
text Given the settings in the previous section, this all becomes:
text Customer name:  Telephone:   Email address:     Pizza Size      Small      Medium      Large     Pizza Toppings     Bacon     Extra Cheese     Onion     Mushroom   Preferred delivery time:      Delivery instructions:  Submit order
