text The  attribute may be specified to provide user agents with a hint of what file types will be accepted.
text accept
text If specified, the attribute must consist of a , each of which must be an  match for one of the following:
row
  link "set of comma-separated tokens" #set-of-comma-separated-tokens
  link "ASCII case-insensitive" https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-case-insensitive
text "The string \"audio/*\""
text Indicates that sound files are accepted.
text "The string \"video/*\""
text Indicates that video files are accepted.
text "The string \"image/*\""
text Indicates that image files are accepted.
text A
link "valid MIME type string with no parameters" https://mimesniff.spec.whatwg.org/#valid-mime-type-with-no-parameters
text Indicates that files of the specified type are accepted.
text A string whose first character is a U+002E FULL STOP character (.)
text Indicates that files with the specified file extension are accepted.
text The tokens must not be  matches for any of the other tokens (i.e. duplicates are not allowed). To obtain the list of tokens from the attribute, the user agent must .
link "ASCII case-insensitive" https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-case-insensitive
link "split the attribute value on commas" https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#split-on-commas
text User agents may use the value of this attribute to display a more appropriate user interface than a generic file picker. For instance, given the value image/*, a user agent could offer the user the option of using a local camera or selecting a photograph from their photo collection; given the value audio/*, a user agent could offer the user the option of recording a clip using a headset microphone.
text User agents should prevent the user from selecting files that are not accepted by one (or more) of these tokens.
text Authors are encouraged to specify both any MIME types and any corresponding extensions when looking for data in a specific format. success
text For example, consider an application that converts Microsoft Word documents to Open Document Format files. Since Microsoft Word documents are described with a wide variety of MIME types and extensions, the site can list several, as follows:
card
