text : User agents must parse the , , , , , and  attributes using the .
text User agent requirements
link min #attr-meter-min
link max #attr-meter-max
link value #attr-meter-value
link low #attr-meter-low
link high #attr-meter-high
link optimum #attr-meter-optimum
link "rules for parsing floating-point number values" #rules-for-parsing-floating-point-number-values
text User agents must then use all these numbers to obtain values for six points on the gauge, as follows. (The order in which these are evaluated is important, as some of the values refer to earlier ones.)
text The
text minimum value
text If the  attribute is specified and a value could be parsed out of it, then the minimum value is that value. Otherwise, the minimum value is zero.
link min #attr-meter-min
text The
text maximum value
text If the  attribute is specified and a value could be parsed out of it, then the candidate maximum value is that value. Otherwise, the candidate maximum value is 1.0.
link max #attr-meter-max
text If the candidate maximum value is greater than or equal to the minimum value, then the maximum value is the candidate maximum value. Otherwise, the maximum value is the same as the minimum value.
text The
text actual value
text If the  attribute is specified and a value could be parsed out of it, then that value is the candidate actual value. Otherwise, the candidate actual value is zero.
link value #attr-meter-value
text If the candidate actual value is less than the minimum value, then the actual value is the minimum value.
text Otherwise, if the candidate actual value is greater than the maximum value, then the actual value is the maximum value.
text Otherwise, the actual value is the candidate actual value.
text The
text low boundary
text If the  attribute is specified and a value could be parsed out of it, then the candidate low boundary is that value. Otherwise, the candidate low boundary is the same as the minimum value.
link low #attr-meter-low
text If the candidate low boundary is less than the minimum value, then the low boundary is the minimum value.
text Otherwise, if the candidate low boundary is greater than the maximum value, then the low boundary is the maximum value.
text Otherwise, the low boundary is the candidate low boundary.
text The
text high boundary
text If the  attribute is specified and a value could be parsed out of it, then the candidate high boundary is that value. Otherwise, the candidate high boundary is the same as the maximum value.
link high #attr-meter-high
text If the candidate high boundary is less than the low boundary, then the high boundary is the low boundary.
text Otherwise, if the candidate high boundary is greater than the maximum value, then the high boundary is the maximum value.
text Otherwise, the high boundary is the candidate high boundary.
text The
text optimum point
text If the  attribute is specified and a value could be parsed out of it, then the candidate optimum point is that value. Otherwise, the candidate optimum point is the midpoint between the minimum value and the maximum value.
link optimum #attr-meter-optimum
text If the candidate optimum point is less than the minimum value, then the optimum point is the minimum value.
text Otherwise, if the candidate optimum point is greater than the maximum value, then the optimum point is the maximum value.
text Otherwise, the optimum point is the candidate optimum point.
text All of which will result in the following inequalities all being true:
text minimum value ≤ actual value ≤ maximum value
text minimum value ≤ low boundary ≤ high boundary ≤ maximum value
text minimum value ≤ optimum point ≤ maximum value
text UA requirements for regions of the gauge: If the optimum point is equal to the low boundary or the high boundary, or anywhere in between them, then the region between the low and high boundaries of the gauge must be treated as the optimum region, and the low and high parts, if any, must be treated as suboptimal. Otherwise, if the optimum point is less than the low boundary, then the region between the minimum value and the low boundary must be treated as the optimum region, the region from the low boundary up to the high boundary must be treated as a suboptimal region, and the remaining region must be treated as an even less good region. Finally, if the optimum point is higher than the high boundary, then the situation is reversed; the region between the high boundary and the maximum value must be treated as the optimum region, the region from the high boundary down to the low boundary must be treated as a suboptimal region, and the remaining region must be treated as an even less good region.
text : When representing a  element to the user, the UA should indicate the relative position of the actual value to the minimum and maximum values, and the relationship between the actual value and the three regions of the gauge.
