h3 4.12.5.7 Premultiplied alpha and the 2D rendering context
text refers to one way of representing transparency in an image, the other being non-premultiplied alpha.
text Premultiplied alpha
text Under non-premultiplied alpha, the red, green, and blue components of a pixel represent that pixel's color, and its alpha component represents that pixel's opacity.
text Under premultiplied alpha, however, the red, green, and blue components of a pixel represent the amounts of color that the pixel adds to the image, and its alpha component represents the amount that the pixel obscures whatever is behind it.
text For instance, assuming the color components range from 0 (off) to 255 (full intensity), these example colors are represented in the following ways:
table "CSS color representation" "Premultiplied representation" "Non-premultiplied representation" "Description of color" "Image of color blended above other content"
  rgba(255, 127, 0, 1) | 255, 127, 0, 255 | 255, 127, 0, 255 | Completely-opaque orange | 
  rgba(255, 255, 0, 0.5) | 127, 127, 0, 127 | 255, 255, 0, 127 | Halfway-opaque yellow | 
  Unrepresentable | 255, 127, 0, 127 | Unrepresentable | Additive halfway-opaque orange | 
  Unrepresentable | 255, 127, 0, 0 | Unrepresentable | Additive fully-transparent orange | 
  rgba(255, 127, 0, 0) | 0, 0, 0, 0 | 255, 127, 0, 0 | Fully-transparent ("invisible") orange | 
  rgba(0, 127, 255, 0) | 0, 0, 0, 0 | 255, 127, 0, 0 | Fully-transparent ("invisible") turquoise | 
text "involves multiplying the color's red, green, and blue components by its alpha component (remapping the range of the alpha component such that \"fully transparent\" is 0, and \"fully opaque\" is 1)."
text Converting a color value from a non-premultiplied representation to a premultiplied one
text involves the inverse: dividing the color's red, green, and blue components by its alpha component.
text Converting a color value from a premultiplied representation to a non-premultiplied one
text "As certain colors can only be represented under premultiplied alpha (for instance, additive colors), and others can only be represented under non-premultiplied alpha (for instance, \"invisible\" colors which hold certain red, green, and blue values even with no opacity); and division and multiplication using finite precision entails a loss of accuracy, converting between premultiplied and non-premultiplied alpha is a lossy operation on colors that are not fully opaque."
text A 's  and an 's  must use premultiplied alpha to represent transparent colors.
row
  link CanvasRenderingContext2D #canvasrenderingcontext2d
  link "output bitmap" #output-bitmap
  link OffscreenCanvasRenderingContext2D #offscreencanvasrenderingcontext2d
  link "output bitmap" #output-bitmap
text It is important for canvas bitmaps to represent colors using premultiplied alpha because it affects the range of representable colors. While additive colors cannot currently be drawn onto canvases directly because CSS colors are non-premultiplied and cannot represent them, it is still possible to, for instance, draw additive colors onto a WebGL canvas and then draw that WebGL canvas onto a 2D canvas via . success
link drawImage() #dom-context-2d-drawimage
h3 4.13 Custom elements
button ✔MDN
h3 4.13.1 Introduction
text This section is non-normative.
text provide a way for authors to build their own fully-featured DOM elements. Although authors could always use non-standard elements in their documents, with application-specific behavior added after the fact by scripting or similar, such elements have historically been non-conforming and not very functional. By  a custom element, authors can inform the parser how to properly construct an element and how elements of that class should react to changes.
link "Custom elements" #custom-element
link defining #element-definition
text "Custom elements are part of a larger effort to \"rationalise the platform\", by explaining existing platform features (like the elements of HTML) in terms of lower-level author-exposed extensibility points (like custom element definition). Although today there are many limitations on the capabilities of custom elements—both functionally and semantically—that prevent them from fully explaining the behaviors of HTML's existing elements, we hope to shrink this gap over time."
