h3 4.13.1.5 Drawbacks of autonomous custom elements
text This section is non-normative.
text As specified below, and alluded to above, simply defining and using an element called  does not mean that such elements  buttons. That is, tools such as web browsers, search engines, or accessibility technology will not automatically treat the resulting element as a button just based on its defined name.
row
  text taco-button
  link represent #represents
text To convey the desired button semantics to a variety of users, while still using an , a number of techniques would need to be employed:
link "autonomous custom element" #autonomous-custom-element
text The addition of the  attribute would make the  . Note that if the  were to become logically disabled, the  attribute would need to be removed.
link tabindex #attr-tabindex
text taco-button
link focusable #focusable
text taco-button
link tabindex #attr-tabindex
text "The addition of an ARIA role and various ARIA states and properties helps convey semantics to accessibility technology. For example, setting the  to \"\" will convey the semantics that this is a button, enabling users to successfully interact with the control using usual button-like interactions in their accessibility technology. Setting the  property is necessary to give the button an , instead of having accessibility technology traverse its child text nodes and announce them. And setting the  state to \"\" when the button is logically disabled conveys to accessibility technology the button's disabled state."
link role https://w3c.github.io/aria/#dfn-role
link button https://w3c.github.io/aria/#button
link aria-label https://w3c.github.io/aria/#aria-label
link "accessible name" https://w3c.github.io/accname/#dfn-accessible-name
link aria-disabled https://w3c.github.io/aria/#aria-disabled
text true
text The addition of event handlers to handle commonly-expected button behaviors helps convey the semantics of the button to web browser users. In this case, the most relevant event handler would be one that proxies appropriate  events to become  events, so that you can activate the button both with keyboard and by clicking.
row
  link keydown https://w3c.github.io/uievents/#event-type-keydown
  link click https://w3c.github.io/pointerevents/#click
text In addition to any default visual styling provided for taco-button elements, the visual styling will also need to be updated to reflect changes in logical state, such as becoming disabled; that is, whatever style sheet has rules for taco-button will also need to have rules for taco-button[disabled].
text With these points in mind, a full-featured taco-button that took on the responsibility of conveying button semantics (including the ability to be disabled) might look something like this:
