  text >
  text </
  text figure success
  text >
text "Both have a figure with a caption, and both, completely unrelated to the figure, have an item with a name-value pair with the name \"name\" and the value \"The Castle\". The only difference is that if the user drags the caption out of the document, in the former case, the item will be included in the drag-and-drop data. In neither case is the image in any way associated with the item."
h3 5.1.3 Typed items
text This section is non-normative.
text The examples in the previous section show how information could be marked up on a page that doesn't expect its microdata to be re-used. Microdata is most useful, though, when it is used in contexts where other authors and readers are able to cooperate to make new uses of the markup.
text "For this purpose, it is necessary to give each  a type, such as \"https://example.com/person\", or \"https://example.org/cat\", or \"https://band.example.net/\". Types are identified as ."
link item #concept-item
link URLs https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url
text The type for an  is given as the value of an  attribute on the same element as the  attribute.
row
  link item #concept-item
  link itemtype #attr-itemtype
  link itemscope #attr-itemscope
text "Here, the item's type is \"https://example.org/animals#cat\":"
text Hedral  Hedral is a male american domestic shorthair, with a fluffy black fur with white paws and belly.
card
  text <
  text section success
  text itemscope primary
  text itemtype primary
  text =
  text "\"https://example.org/animals#cat\"" warning
  text >
  text <
  text h1 success
  text itemprop primary
  text =
  text "\"name\"" warning
  text >
  text </
  text h1 success
  text >
  text <
  text p success
  text itemprop primary
  text =
  text "\"desc\"" warning
  text >
  text </
  text p success
  text >
  text <
  text img success
  text itemprop primary
  text =
  text "\"img\"" warning
  text src primary
  text =
  text "\"hedral.jpeg\"" warning
  text alt primary
  text =
  text "\"\"" warning
  text title primary
  text =
  text "\"Hedral, age 18 months\"" warning
  text >
  text </
  text section success
  text >
text "In this example the \"https://example.org/animals#cat\" item has three properties, a \"name\" (\"Hedral\"), a \"desc\" (\"Hedral is...\"), and an \"img\" (\"hedral.jpeg\")."
text "The type gives the context for the properties, thus selecting a vocabulary: a property named \"class\" given for an item with the type \"https://census.example/person\" might refer to the economic class of an individual, while a property named \"class\" given for an item with the type \"https://example.com/school/teacher\" might refer to the classroom a teacher has been assigned. Several types can share a vocabulary. For example, the types \"https://example.org/people/teacher\" and \"https://example.org/people/engineer\" could be defined to use the same vocabulary (though maybe some properties would not be especially useful in both cases, e.g. maybe the \"https://example.org/people/engineer\" type might not typically be used with the \"classroom\" property). Multiple types defined to use the same vocabulary can be given for a single item by listing the URLs as a space-separated list in the attribute' value. An item cannot be given two types if they do not use the same vocabulary, however."
h3 5.1.4 Global identifiers for items
text This section is non-normative.
text Sometimes, an  gives information about a topic that has a global identifier. For example, books can be identified by their ISBN number.
link item #concept-item
text Vocabularies (as identified by the  attribute) can be designed such that  get associated with their global identifier in an unambiguous way by expressing the global identifiers as  given in an  attribute.
link itemtype #attr-itemtype
link items #concept-item
link URLs https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url
link itemid #attr-itemid
