  link URL https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url
h3 5.1.5 Selecting names when defining vocabularies
text This section is non-normative.
text Using microdata means using a vocabulary. For some purposes, an ad-hoc vocabulary is adequate. For others, a vocabulary will need to be designed. Where possible, authors are encouraged to re-use existing vocabularies, as this makes content re-use easier.
text When designing new vocabularies, identifiers can be created either using , or, for properties, as plain words (with no dots or colons). For URLs, conflicts with other vocabularies can be avoided by only using identifiers that correspond to pages that the author has control over.
link URLs https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url
text "For instance, if Jon and Adam both write content at example.com, at https://example.com/~jon/... and https://example.com/~adam/... respectively, then they could select identifiers of the form \"https://example.com/~jon/name\" and \"https://example.com/~adam/name\" respectively."
text Properties whose names are just plain words can only be used within the context of the types for which they are intended; properties named using URLs can be reused in items of any type. If an item has no type, and is not part of another item, then if its properties have names that are just plain words, they are not intended to be globally unique, and are instead only intended for limited use. Generally speaking, authors are encouraged to use either properties with globally unique names (URLs) or ensure that their items are typed.
text "Here, an item is an \"https://example.org/animals#cat\", and most of the properties have names that are words defined in the context of that type. There are also a few additional properties whose names come from other vocabularies."
text Hedral  Hedral is a male American domestic shorthair, with a fluffy  black fur with  white paws and belly.
card
  text <
  text section success
  text itemscope primary
  text itemtype primary
  text =
  text "\"https://example.org/animals#cat\"" warning
  text >
  text <
  text h1 success
  text itemprop primary
  text =
  text "\"name https://example.com/fn\"" warning
  text >
  text </
  text h1 success
  text >
  text <
  text p success
  text itemprop primary
  text =
  text "\"desc\"" warning
  text >
  text <
  text span success
  text itemprop primary
  text =
  text "\"https://example.com/color\"" warning
  text >
  text </
  text span success
  text >
  text <
  text span success
  text itemprop primary
  text =
  text "\"https://example.com/color\"" warning
  text >
  text </
  text span success
  text >
  text </
  text p success
  text >
  text <
  text img success
  text itemprop primary
  text =
  text "\"img\"" warning
  text src primary
  text =
  text "\"hedral.jpeg\"" warning
  text alt primary
  text =
  text "\"\"" warning
  text title primary
  text =
  text "\"Hedral, age 18 months\"" warning
  text >
  text </
  text section success
  text >
text "This example has one item with the type \"https://example.org/animals#cat\" and the following properties:"
table Property Value
  name | Hedral
  https://example.com/fn | Hedral
  desc | Hedral is a male American domestic shorthair, with a fluffy black fur with white paws and belly.
  https://example.com/color | black
  https://example.com/color | white
  img | .../hedral.jpeg
h3 5.2 Encoding microdata
h3 5.2.1 The microdata model
text The microdata model consists of groups of name-value pairs known as .
link items #concept-item
text Each group is known as an . Each  can have , a  (if the vocabulary specified by the  ), and a list of name-value pairs. Each name in the name-value pair is known as a , and each  has one or more . Each  is either a string or itself a group of name-value pairs (an ). The names are unordered relative to each other, but if a particular name has multiple values, they do have a relative order.
