text The  attribute is not part of the microdata data model. It is merely a syntactic construct to aid authors in adding annotations to pages where the data to be annotated does not follow a convenient tree structure. For example, it allows authors to mark up data in a table so that each column defines a separate , while keeping the properties in the cells. success
link itemref #attr-itemref
link item #concept-item
text This example shows a simple vocabulary used to describe the products of a model railway manufacturer. The vocabulary has just five property names:
text product-code
text An integer that names the product in the manufacturer's catalog.
text name
text A brief description of the product.
text scale
text "One of \"HO\", \"1\", or \"Z\" (potentially with leading or trailing whitespace), indicating the scale of the product."
text digital
text "If present, one of \"Digital\", \"Delta\", or \"Systems\" (potentially with leading or trailing whitespace) indicating that the product has a digital decoder of the given type."
text track-type
text "For track-specific products, one of \"K\", \"M\", \"C\" (potentially with leading or trailing whitespace) indicating the type of track for which the product is intended."
text This vocabulary has four defined :
link "item types" #item-types
text "https://md.example.com/loco"
text Rolling stock with an engine
text "https://md.example.com/passengers"
text Passenger rolling stock
text "https://md.example.com/track"
text Track pieces
text "https://md.example.com/lighting"
text Equipment with lighting
text Each  that uses this vocabulary can be given one or more of these types, depending on what the product is.
link item #concept-item
text Thus, a locomotive might be marked up as:
text Name:  Tank Locomotive (DB 80) Product code:  33041 Scale:  HO Digital:  Delta
