h3 6.8.3 Best practices for in-page editors
text Authors are encouraged to set the  property on  and on markup that was originally created through these editing mechanisms to the value 'pre-wrap'. Default HTML whitespace handling is not well suited to WYSIWYG editing, and line wrapping will not work correctly in some corner cases if  is left at its default value.
link 'white-space' https://drafts.csswg.org/css-text/#white-space-property
link "editing hosts" #editing-host
link 'white-space' https://drafts.csswg.org/css-text/#white-space-property
text "As an example of problems that occur if the default 'normal' value is used instead, consider the case of the user typing \"\", with two spaces (here represented by \"␣\") between the words. With the editing rules in place for the default value of  ('normal'), the resulting markup will either consist of \"\" or \"\"; i.e., there will be a non-breaking space between the two words in addition to the regular space. This is necessary because the 'normal' value for  requires adjacent regular spaces to be collapsed together."
text yellow␣␣ball
link 'white-space' https://drafts.csswg.org/css-text/#white-space-property
text yellow&nbsp; ball
text yellow &nbsp;ball
link 'white-space' https://drafts.csswg.org/css-text/#white-space-property
text "In the former case, \"\" might wrap to the next line (\"⍽\" being used here to represent a non-breaking space) even though \"\" alone might fit at the end of the line; in the latter case, \"\", if wrapped to the start of the line, would have visible indentation from the non-breaking space."
text yellow⍽
text yellow
text ⍽ball
text When  is set to 'pre-wrap', however, the editing rules will instead simply put two regular spaces between the words, and should the two words be split at the end of a line, the spaces would be neatly removed from the rendering.
link 'white-space' https://drafts.csswg.org/css-text/#white-space-property
