text Most of the complexity of the  comes from anti-abuse protections designed to prevent developers from disabling users' history traversal abilities, for platforms where a 's  is the main mechanism of history traversal. In particular:
link "close watcher manager" #close-watcher-manager
link "close request" #close-request
link "fallback action" #close-request-fallback
text The grouping of  is designed so that if multiple close watchers are created without , they are grouped together, so that a user-triggered  will close all of the close watchers in a group. This ensures that web developers can't intercept an unlimited number of close requests by creating close watchers; instead they can create a number equal to at most 1 + the number of times the .
link "close watchers" #close-watcher
link "history-action activation" #history-action-activation
link "close request" #close-request
link "user activates the page" #tracking-user-activation
text "The  boolean encourages web developers to create  in a way that is tied to individual . Without it, each user activation would increase the , even if the web developer isn't \"using\" those user activations to create close watchers. In short:"
link "next user interaction allows a new group" #close-watcher-manager-next
link "close watchers" #close-watcher
link "user activations" #tracking-user-activation
link "allowed number of groups" #close-watcher-manager-allowed-number-of-groups
text Allowed: user interaction; create a close watcher in its own group; user interaction; create a close watcher in a second independent group.
text Disallowed: user interaction; user interaction; create a close watcher in its own group; create a close watcher in a second independent group.
text Allowed: user interaction; user interaction; create a close watcher in its own group; create a close watcher grouped with the previous one.
text "This protection is  important for upholding our desired invariant of creating at most (1 + the number of times the ) groups. A determined abuser will just create one close watcher per user interaction, \"banking\" them for future abuse. But this system causes more predictable behavior for the normal case, and encourages non-abusive developers to create close watchers directly in response to user interactions."
