h3 8.1.5.2 Import maps
text "An  allows control over module specifier resolution. Import maps are delivered via inline  elements with their  attribute set to \"\", and with their  containing a JSON representation of the import map."
link "import map" #import-map
link script #the-script-element
link type #attr-script-type
text importmap
link "child text content" https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-child-text-content
text "A  can have multiple import maps processed, which can happen either before or after any modules have been imported, e.g., via  expressions or  elements with their  attribute set to \"\". The  algorithm ensures that new import maps cannot define the module resolution for modules that were already defined by past import maps, or for ones that were already resolved."
link Document #document
link import() https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-import-calls
link script #the-script-element
link type #attr-script-type
text module
link "merge existing and new import maps" #merge-existing-and-new-import-maps
text The simplest use of import maps is to globally remap a bare module specifier:
card
  text {
  text "\"imports\"" warning
  text :
  text {
  text "\"moment\"" warning
  text :
  text "\"/node_modules/moment/src/moment.js\"" warning
  text }
  text }
text "This enables statements like import moment from \"moment\"; to work, fetching and evaluating the JavaScript module at the /node_modules/moment/src/moment.js URL."
text An import map can remap a class of module specifiers into a class of URLs by using trailing slashes, like so:
card
  text {
  text "\"imports\"" warning
  text :
  text {
  text "\"moment/\"" warning
  text :
  text "\"/node_modules/moment/src/\"" warning
  text }
  text }
text "This enables statements like import localeData from \"moment/locale/zh-cn.js\"; to work, fetching and evaluating the JavaScript module at the /node_modules/moment/src/locale/zh-cn.js URL. Such trailing-slash mappings are often combined with bare-specifier mappings, e.g."
card
  text {
  text "\"imports\"" warning
  text :
  text {
  text "\"moment\"" warning
  text :
  text "\"/node_modules/moment/src/moment.js\"" warning
  text ,
  text "\"moment/\"" warning
  text :
  text "\"/node_modules/moment/src/\"" warning
  text }
  text }
text "so that both the \"main module\" specified by \"moment\" and the \"submodules\" specified by paths such as \"moment/locale/zh-cn.js\" are available."
text "Bare specifiers are not the only type of module specifiers which import maps can remap. \"URL-like\" specifiers, i.e., those that are either parseable as absolute URLs or start with \"/\", \"./\", or \"../\", can be remapped as well:"
card
  text {
  text "\"imports\"" warning
  text :
  text {
  text "\"https://cdn.example.com/vue/dist/vue.runtime.esm.js\"" warning
  text :
  text "\"/node_modules/vue/dist/vue.runtime.esm.js\"" warning
  text ,
  text "\"/js/app.mjs\"" warning
  text :
  text "\"/js/app-8e0d62a03.mjs\"" warning
  text ,
  text "\"../helpers/\"" warning
  text :
  text "\"https://cdn.example/helpers/\"" warning
  text }
  text }
text "Note how the URL to be remapped, as well as the URL being mapped to, can be specified either as absolute URLs, or as relative URLs starting with \"\", \"\", or \"\". (They cannot be specified as relative URLs without those starting sigils, as those help distinguish from bare module specifiers.) Also note how the  works in this context as well."
text /
text ./
text ../
link "trailing slash mapping" #example-import-map-trailing-slashes
text "Such remappings operate on the post-canonicalization URL, and do not require a match between the literal strings supplied in the import map key and the imported module specifier. So for example, if this import map was included on https://example.com/app.html, then not only would import \"/js/app.mjs\" be remapped, but so would import \"./js/app.mjs\" and import \"./foo/../js/app.mjs\"."
text "All previous examples have globally remapped module specifiers, by using the top-level \"imports\" key in the import map. The top-level \"scopes\" key can be used to provide localized remappings, which only apply when the referring module matches a specific URL prefix. For example:"
card
  text {
  text "\"scopes\"" warning
  text :
  text {
  text "\"/a/\"" warning
  text :
  text {
  text "\"moment\"" warning
  text :
  text "\"/node_modules/moment/src/moment.js\"" warning
  text },
  text "\"/b/\"" warning
  text :
  text {
  text "\"moment\"" warning
  text :
  text "\"https://cdn.example.com/moment/src/moment.js\"" warning
  text }
  text }
  text }
text "With this import map, the statement import \"moment\" will have different meanings depending on which referrer script contains the statement:"
text Inside scripts located under /a/, this will import /node_modules/moment/src/moment.js.
text "Inside scripts located under /b/, this will import https://cdn.example.com/moment/src/moment.js."
text Inside scripts located under /c/, this will fail to resolve and thus throw an exception.
text "A typical usage of scopes is to allow multiple versions of the \"same\" module to exist in a web application, with some parts of the module graph importing one version, and other parts importing another version."
text "Scopes can overlap each other, and overlap the global \"\" specifier map. At resolution time, scopes are consulted in order of most- to least-specific, where specificity is measured by sorting the scopes using the  operation. So, for example, \"\" is treated as more specific than \"\", which is treated as more specific than the top-level (unscoped) mappings."
text imports
link "code unit less than" https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit-less-than
text /scope2/scope3/
text /scope2/
text The following import map illustrates this:
card
  text {
  text "\"imports\"" warning
  text :
  text {
  text "\"a\"" warning
  text :
  text "\"/a-1.mjs\"" warning
  text ,
  text "\"b\"" warning
  text :
  text "\"/b-1.mjs\"" warning
  text ,
  text "\"c\"" warning
  text :
  text "\"/c-1.mjs\"" warning
  text },
  text "\"scopes\"" warning
  text :
  text {
  text "\"/scope2/\"" warning
  text :
  text {
  text "\"a\"" warning
  text :
  text "\"/a-2.mjs\"" warning
  text },
  text "\"/scope2/scope3/\"" warning
  text :
  text {
  text "\"b\"" warning
  text :
  text "\"/b-3.mjs\"" warning
  text }
  text }
  text }
text This results in the following resolutions (using relative URLs for brevity):
table Col Specifier
  "a" | "b" | "c"
  Referrer | /scope1/r.mjs | /a-1.mjs | /b-1.mjs | /c-1.mjs
  /scope2/r.mjs | /a-2.mjs | /b-1.mjs | /c-1.mjs
  /scope2/scope3/r.mjs | /a-2.mjs | /b-3.mjs | /c-1.mjs
text Import maps can also be used to provide modules with integrity metadata to be used in  checks.
row
