h3 8.6.6 Security considerations
text This section is non-normative.
text The Sanitizer API is intended to prevent DOM-based cross-site scripting by removing elements and attributes according to a configuration as supplied HTML content is parsed. By design, the  and  methods remove script-capable markup regardless of the configuration supplied; if any configuration could preserve such markup through these methods, that would be a bug.
row
  link setHTML() #dom-element-sethtml
  link parseHTML() #dom-parsehtml
text By contrast, unsafe HTML parsing methods (such as  and ) and legacy HTML sinks (such as , , and ) only sanitize when a sanitizer is supplied via options or a Trusted Types default policy's  callback. Because a default policy's  callback is invoked for these sinks instead of , defining a  callback that passes options through without a sanitizer will leave  and related sinks unsanitized even if the policy's  callback sanitizes strings. Similarly, enabling  in  will cause scripts inserted via , , and  to execute.
link setHTMLUnsafe() #dom-element-sethtmlunsafe
link parseHTMLUnsafe() #dom-parsehtmlunsafe
link innerHTML #dom-element-innerhtml
link outerHTML #dom-element-outerhtml
link insertAdjacentHTML() #dom-element-insertadjacenthtml
text createParserOptions
text createParserOptions
text createHTML
text createParserOptions
link innerHTML #dom-element-innerhtml
text createHTML
link runScripts #dom-sethtmlunsafeoptions-runscripts
text createParserOptions
link innerHTML #dom-element-innerhtml
link outerHTML #dom-element-outerhtml
link insertAdjacentHTML() #dom-element-insertadjacenthtml
text However, there are security issues that the Sanitizer API cannot prevent. The following sections describe them.
h3 8.6.6.1 Server-side reflected and stored XSS
text This section is non-normative.
text The Sanitizer API operates solely in the DOM and adds a capability to filter HTML content as it is parsed into a node tree. The Sanitizer API does not address server-side reflected or stored XSS.
h3 8.6.6.2 DOM clobbering
text This section is non-normative.
text DOM clobbering describes an attack in which malicious HTML confuses an application by using  or  attributes such that DOM properties, such as the  property of an HTML element, are shadowed by malicious content.
link id #the-id-attribute
text name
link children https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-parentnode-children
text The Sanitizer API does not protect against DOM clobbering attacks by default, but can be configured to remove  and  attributes.
row
  link id #the-id-attribute
  text name
h3 8.6.6.3 XSS with script gadgets
text This section is non-normative.
text Script gadgets are a technique in which an attacker uses existing application code from popular JavaScript libraries to cause their own code to execute. This is often done by injecting innocent-looking code or seemingly inert DOM nodes that are only parsed and interpreted by a framework which then performs the execution of JavaScript based on that input.
text The Sanitizer API cannot prevent these attacks. Instead, it relies on authors to explicitly allow unknown elements in general, and additionally to explicitly allow attributes, elements, and markup commonly used for templating and framework-specific code, such as  and  attributes and elements like  and . These restrictions are not exhaustive and authors are encouraged to examine their third party libraries for this behavior.
link data-* #attr-data-*
link slot #attr-slot-name
link slot #the-slot-element
link template #the-template-element
h3 8.6.6.4 Mutation XSS
text This section is non-normative.
text Mutation XSS or mXSS describes an attack that exploits cases where the parsed DOM structure is not the same after serializing and parsing again, to bypass sanitization that happens before serialization. An example for carrying out such an attack is by relying on the change of parsing behavior for foreign content or mis-nested tags.
text The Sanitizer API offers only functions that turn a string into a node tree. The context is supplied implicitly by all sanitizer functions: , , and  use the element they're called on (or, for a , its ); , , and  use the parent (or, for a  or , its ); and  creates a new document. Therefore Sanitizer API is not directly affected by mutation XSS.
link setHTML() #dom-element-sethtml
link appendHTML() #dom-element-appendhtml
link prependHTML() #dom-element-prependhtml
