text This script first checks the domain is the expected domain, and then looks at the message, which it either displays to the user, or responds to by sending a message back to the document which sent the message in the first place.
h3 9.3.2 Security
h3 9.3.2.1 Authors
text Use of this API requires extra care to protect users from hostile entities abusing a site for their own purposes. danger
text Authors should check the  attribute to ensure that messages are only accepted from domains that they expect to receive messages from. Otherwise, bugs in the author's message handling code could be exploited by hostile sites.
link origin #dom-messageevent-origin
text Furthermore, even after checking the  attribute, authors should also check that the data in question is of the expected format. Otherwise, if the source of the event has been attacked using a cross-site scripting flaw, further unchecked processing of information sent using the  method could result in the attack being propagated into the receiver.
link origin #dom-messageevent-origin
link postMessage() #dom-window-postmessage
text Authors should not use the wildcard keyword (*) in the  argument in messages that contain any confidential information, as otherwise there is no way to guarantee that the message is only delivered to the recipient to which it was intended.
text targetOrigin
text Authors who accept messages from any origin are encouraged to consider the risks of a denial-of-service attack. An attacker could send a high volume of messages; if the receiving page performs expensive computation or causes network traffic to be sent for each such message, the attacker's message could be multiplied into a denial-of-service attack. Authors are encouraged to employ rate limiting (only accepting a certain number of messages per minute) to make such attacks impractical.
h3 9.3.2.2 User agents
text The integrity of this API is based on the inability for scripts of one  to post arbitrary events (using  or otherwise) to objects in other origins (those that are not the ).
link origin #concept-origin
text dispatchEvent()
link same #same-origin
text Implementers are urged to take extra care in the implementation of this feature. It allows authors to transmit information from one domain to another domain, which is normally disallowed for security reasons. It also requires that UAs be careful to allow access to certain properties but not others. success
text User agents are also encouraged to consider rate-limiting message traffic between different , to protect naïve sites from denial-of-service attacks.
link origins #concept-origin
