h3 9.4.5 Broadcasting to many ports
text This section is non-normative.
text Broadcasting to many ports is in principle relatively simple: keep an array of  objects to send messages to, and iterate through the array to send a message. However, this has one rather unfortunate effect: it prevents the ports from being garbage collected, even if the other side has gone away. To avoid this problem, implement a simple protocol whereby the other side acknowledges it still exists. If it doesn't do so after a certain amount of time, assume it's gone, close the  object, and let it be garbage collected.
link MessagePort #messageport
link MessagePort #messageport
h3 9.4.6 Ports and garbage collection
text When a  object  is entangled, user agents must either act as if 's entangled  object has a strong reference to , or as if 's  has a strong reference to .
link MessagePort #messageport
text o
text o
link MessagePort #messageport
text o
text o
link "relevant global object" #concept-relevant-global
text o
text Thus, a message port can be received, given an event listener, and then forgotten, and so long as that event listener could receive a message, the channel will be maintained. success
text Of course, if this was to occur on both sides of the channel, then both ports could be garbage collected, since they would not be reachable from live code, despite having a strong reference to each other. success
text Furthermore, a  object must not be garbage collected while there exists an event referenced by a  in a  that is to be dispatched on that  object, or while the  object's  is enabled and not empty.
link MessagePort #messageport
link task #concept-task
link "task queue" #task-queue
link MessagePort #messageport
link MessagePort #messageport
link "port message queue" #port-message-queue
text Authors are strongly encouraged to explicitly close  objects to disentangle them, so that their resources can be recollected. Creating many  objects and discarding them without closing them can lead to high transient memory usage since garbage collection is not necessarily performed promptly, especially for s where garbage collection can involve cross-process coordination. success
