h3 11.3.1.1 Agents and event loops
text This section is non-normative.
text Each  is contained in its own , which has its corresponding . However, in practice, implementation of these agents and event loops is expected to be different from most others.
row
  link WorkletGlobalScope #workletglobalscope
  link "worklet agent" #worklet-agent
  link "event loop" #concept-agent-event-loop
text "A  exists for each  since, in theory, an implementation could use a separate thread for each  instance, and allowing this level of parallelism is best done using agents. However, because their [[CanBlock]] value is false, there is no requirement that agents and threads are one-to-one. This allows implementations the freedom to execute scripts loaded into a worklet on any thread, including one running code from other agents with [[CanBlock]] of false, such as the thread of a  (\"the main thread\"). Contrast this with , whose true value for [[CanBlock]] effectively requires them to get a dedicated operating system thread."
link "worklet agent" #worklet-agent
link WorkletGlobalScope #workletglobalscope
link WorkletGlobalScope #workletglobalscope
link "similar-origin window agent" #similar-origin-window-agent
link "dedicated worker agents" #dedicated-worker-agent
text Worklet  are also somewhat special. They are only used for  associated with , tasks wherein the user agent invokes author-defined methods, and . Thus, even though the  specifies that all event loops run continuously, implementations can achieve observably-equivalent results using a simpler strategy, which just  author-provided methods and then relies on that process to .
