h2 12 Web storage
button ✔MDN
h3 12.1 Introduction
text This section is non-normative.
text This specification introduces two related mechanisms, similar to HTTP session cookies, for storing name-value pairs on the client side.
link [COOKIES] #refsCOOKIES
text The first is designed for scenarios where the user is carrying out a single transaction, but could be carrying out multiple transactions in different windows at the same time.
text "Cookies don't really handle this case well. For example, a user could be buying plane tickets in two different windows, using the same site. If the site used cookies to keep track of which ticket the user was buying, then as the user clicked from page to page in both windows, the ticket currently being purchased would \"leak\" from one window to the other, potentially causing the user to buy two tickets for the same flight without noticing."
text To address this, this specification introduces the  getter. Sites can add data to the session storage, and it will be accessible to any page from the same site opened in that window.
link sessionStorage #dom-sessionstorage
text For example, a page could have a checkbox that the user ticks to indicate that they want insurance:
text I want insurance on this trip.
card
  text <
  text label success
  text >
  text <
  text input success
  text type primary
  text =
  text "\"checkbox\"" warning
  text onchange primary
  text =
  text "\"sessionStorage.insurance = checked ? 'true' : ''\"" warning
  text >
  text </
  text label success
  text >
text A later page could then check, from script, whether the user had checked the checkbox or not:
text sessionStorageinsurance
card
  text if danger
  text (
  text .
  text )
  text {
  text ...
  text }
text If the user had multiple windows opened on the site, each one would have its own individual copy of the session storage object.
text The second storage mechanism is designed for storage that spans multiple windows, and lasts beyond the current session. In particular, web applications might wish to store megabytes of user data, such as entire user-authored documents or a user's mailbox, on the client side for performance reasons.
text Again, cookies do not handle this case well, because they are transmitted with every request.
text The  getter is used to access a page's local storage area.
link localStorage #dom-localstorage
text The site at example.com can display a count of how many times the user has loaded its page by putting the following at the bottom of its page:
text You have viewed this page  an untold number of time(s).    localStoragepageLoadCount localStoragepageLoadCount   localStoragepageLoadCount  parseIntlocalStoragepageLoadCount   documentgetElementByIdtextContent  localStoragepageLoadCount
