h3 13.2 Parsing HTML documents
text This section only applies to user agents, data mining tools, and conformance checkers.
text "The rules for parsing XML documents into DOM trees are covered by the next section, entitled \"\"." success
link "The XML syntax" #the-xhtml-syntax
text User agents must use the parsing rules described in this section to generate the DOM trees from  resources. Together, these rules define what is referred to as the .
link text/html #text/html
text HTML parser
text While the HTML syntax described in this specification bears a close resemblance to SGML and XML, it is a separate language with its own parsing rules. success
text Some earlier versions of HTML (in particular from HTML2 to HTML4) were based on SGML and used SGML parsing rules. However, few (if any) web browsers ever implemented true SGML parsing for HTML documents; the only user agents to strictly handle HTML as an SGML application have historically been validators. The resulting confusion — with validators claiming documents to have one representation while widely deployed web browsers interoperably implemented a different representation — has wasted decades of productivity. This version of HTML thus returns to a non-SGML basis. success
