h2 17 IANA considerations
h3 17.1 text/html
text This registration is for community review and will be submitted to the IESG for review, approval, and registration with IANA.
text Type name:
text text
text Subtype name:
text html
text Required parameters:
text No required parameters
text Optional parameters:
text charset
text "The  parameter may be provided to specify the , overriding any  in the document other than a Byte Order Mark (BOM). The parameter's value must be an  match for the string \"\"."
text charset
link "document's character encoding" https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-document-encoding
link "character encoding declarations" #character-encoding-declaration
link "ASCII case-insensitive" https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-case-insensitive
text utf-8
link [ENCODING] #refsENCODING
text Encoding considerations:
text 8bit (see the section on )
link "character encoding declarations" #character-encoding-declaration
text Security considerations:
text Entire novels have been written about the security considerations that apply to HTML documents. Many are listed in this document, to which the reader is referred for more details. Some general concerns bear mentioning here, however:
text HTML is scripted language, and has a large number of APIs (some of which are described in this document). Script can expose the user to potential risks of information leakage, credential leakage, cross-site scripting attacks, cross-site request forgeries, and a host of other problems. While the designs in this specification are intended to be safe if implemented correctly, a full implementation is a massive undertaking and, as with any software, user agents are likely to have security bugs.
text Even without scripting, there are specific features in HTML which, for historical reasons, are required for broad compatibility with legacy content but that expose the user to unfortunate security problems. In particular, the  element can be used in conjunction with some other features as a way to effect a port scan from the user's location on the Internet. This can expose local network topologies that the attacker would otherwise not be able to determine.
link img #the-img-element
text HTML relies on a compartmentalization scheme sometimes known as the . An  in most cases consists of all the pages served from the same host, on the same port, using the same protocol.
row
  text same-origin policy
  link origin #concept-origin
text It is critical, therefore, to ensure that any untrusted content that forms part of a site be hosted on a different  than any sensitive content on that site. Untrusted content can easily spoof any other page on the same origin, read data from that origin, cause scripts in that origin to execute, submit forms to and from that origin even if they are protected from cross-site request forgery attacks by unique tokens, and make use of any third-party resources exposed to or rights granted to that origin.
link origin #concept-origin
text Interoperability considerations:
text Rules for processing both conforming and non-conforming content are defined in this specification.
text Published specification:
text This document is the relevant specification. Labeling a resource with the  type asserts that the resource is an  using .
link text/html #text/html
link "HTML document" https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#html-document
link "the HTML syntax" #syntax
text Applications that use this media type:
text Web browsers, tools for processing web content, HTML authoring tools, search engines, validators.
text Additional information:
text Magic number(s):
text No sequence of bytes can uniquely identify an HTML document. More information on detecting HTML documents is available in .
text MIME Sniffing
link [MIMESNIFF] #refsMIMESNIFF
text File extension(s):
text "\"html\" and \"htm\" are commonly, but certainly not exclusively, used as the extension for HTML documents."
text Macintosh file type code(s):
text TEXT
text Person & email address to contact for further information:
text Ian Hickson <ian@hixie.ch>
text Intended usage:
text Common
text Restrictions on usage:
text No restrictions apply.
text Author:
text Ian Hickson <ian@hixie.ch>
text Change controller:
text W3C
text used with  resources either refer to the  of the corresponding , or provide state information for in-page scripts.
row
  link Fragments https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url-fragment
  link text/html #text/html
  link "indicated part" #the-indicated-part-of-the-document
  link Document #document
h3 17.2 multipart/x-mixed-replace
text This registration is for community review and will be submitted to the IESG for review, approval, and registration with IANA.
text Type name:
text multipart
text Subtype name:
text x-mixed-replace
text Required parameters:
text (defined in RFC2046)
row
  text boundary
  link [RFC2046] #refsRFC2046
text Optional parameters:
text No optional parameters.
text Encoding considerations:
text binary
text Security considerations:
