h3 17.7 text/event-stream
text This registration is for community review and will be submitted to the IESG for review, approval, and registration with IANA.
text Type name:
text text
text Subtype name:
text event-stream
text Required parameters:
text No parameters
text Optional parameters:
text charset
text "The charset parameter may be provided. The parameter's value must be \"utf-8\". This parameter serves no purpose; it is only allowed for compatibility with legacy servers."
text Encoding considerations:
text 8bit (always UTF-8)
text Security considerations:
text An event stream from an origin distinct from the origin of the content consuming the event stream can result in information leakage. To avoid this, user agents are required to apply CORS semantics.
link [FETCH] #refsFETCH
text Event streams can overwhelm a user agent; a user agent is expected to apply suitable restrictions to avoid depleting local resources because of an overabundance of information from an event stream.
text Servers can be overwhelmed if a situation develops in which the server is causing clients to reconnect rapidly. Servers should use a 5xx status code to indicate capacity problems, as this will prevent conforming clients from reconnecting automatically.
text Interoperability considerations:
text Rules for processing both conforming and non-conforming content are defined in this specification.
text Published specification:
text This document is the relevant specification.
text Applications that use this media type:
text Web browsers and tools using web services.
text Additional information:
text Magic number(s):
text No sequence of bytes can uniquely identify an event stream.
text File extension(s):
text No specific file extensions are recommended for this type.
text Macintosh file type code(s):
text No specific Macintosh file type codes are recommended for this type.
text Person & email address to contact for further information:
text Ian Hickson <ian@hixie.ch>
text Intended usage:
text Common
text Restrictions on usage:
text This format is only expected to be used by dynamic open-ended streams served using HTTP or a similar protocol. Finite resources are not expected to be labeled with this type.
text Author:
text Ian Hickson <ian@hixie.ch>
text Change controller:
text W3C
text have no meaning with  resources.
row
  link Fragments https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url-fragment
  link text/event-stream #text/event-stream
h3 17.8 web+ scheme prefix
text This section describes a convention for use with the IANA URI scheme registry. It does not itself register a specific scheme.
link [RFC7595] #refsRFC7595
text Scheme name:
text "Schemes starting with the four characters \"web+\" followed by one or more letters in the range a-z."
text Status:
text Permanent
text Scheme syntax:
text Scheme-specific.
text Scheme semantics:
text Scheme-specific.
text Encoding considerations:
text "All \"web+\" schemes should use UTF-8 encodings where relevant."
text Applications/protocols that use this scheme name:
text Scheme-specific.
text Interoperability considerations:
text The scheme is expected to be used in the context of web applications.
text Security considerations:
text "Any web page is able to register a handler for all \"web+\" schemes. As such, these schemes must not be used for features intended to be core platform features (e.g., HTTP). Similarly, such schemes must not store confidential information in their URLs, such as usernames, passwords, personal information, or confidential project names."
text Contact:
text Ian Hickson <ian@hixie.ch>
text Change controller:
text Ian Hickson <ian@hixie.ch>
text References:
text , HTML Living Standard:
row
  text Custom scheme handlers
  link "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#custom-handlers" #custom-handlers
